message: "If you use this dataset or software, please cite it as follows. This deposit is unrelated to DOI 10.5281/zenodo.22849652, which covers a different project."
title: "ScientificDiscoveryLab: Three Instrument Defects Found by Self-Audit"
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: 2026-09-28
abstract: >
  A self-audit of an autonomous simulation laboratory whose principal asset is
  methodological discipline rather than results: preregistrations frozen by
  SHA-256, hash-chained change logs, independent second implementations,
  fail-closed runners, and an explicit do-not-claim register. Three genuine
  defects were found in the laboratory's own instruments. First, a PRNG
  statistical test (NIST SP 800-22 section 2.3) applied a z-score conversion to
  a statistic already defined in erfc units, giving a Kolmogorov-Smirnov p-value
  of 6.7e-06 with 0 of 200 rejections where an independent reference
  implementation of the same section gives 0.847 with 1 of 200 on identical
  bits; the standard's own applicability precondition is met by 0 of 200 seeds,
  so the corrected status is unresolvable and the accurate per-test summary is
  23 of 24 calibrated, 0 miscalibrated, 1 unresolvable. Second, a 2D percolation
  cluster-mass exponent of 1.92009 against the Fisher value 2.054945 was found to
  rest on an estimator biased upward by 0.11 to 0.35, measured on synthetic
  distributions of known exponent; the deviation is not finite-size crossover,
  and correcting the bias moves the exponent further from the Fisher value, so
  the escalation stands strengthened while its numeric value is untrustworthy.
  Third, a reproducibility control that pinned an append-only registry by
  whole-file hash was repaired without refreshing any baseline. The test suite
  went from 391 passed with 10 errors to 495 passed with 0 errors, and every
  historical artifact is verified byte-identical. No scientific discovery is
  claimed.
keywords:
  - reproducibility research
  - measurement instrument validation
  - estimator bias
  - statistical test calibration
  - NIST SP 800-22
  - percolation
  - cluster mass exponent
  - finite-size scaling
  - preregistration
  - hash chaining
  - fail-closed analysis
  - negative results
  - null results
  - self-audit
  - scientific integrity
  - software correctness
